## Method and reasoning
Push opening positions. A closer can only match the most recent unmatched opener. An empty stack or a type mismatch makes the current closer the first error. If scanning finishes, the bottom of the stack contains the earliest still-unmatched opener.

## Walk through the sample
After reading the first four characters of ({[])], the square pair has closed and the top opener is {. Position 5 contains ), which cannot close it. This is the first failure even though an earlier ( exists deeper in the stack.

## Complexity
Time O(n), space O(n).

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    string s;
    cin >> s;
    vector<int> stack;
    for (int i = 0; i < int(s.size()); ++i) {
        char c = s[i];
        if (c == '(' || c == '[' || c == '{')
            stack.push_back(i);
        else {
            char open = c == ')' ? '(' : c == ']' ? '[' : '{';
            if (stack.empty() || s[stack.back()] != open) {
                cout << i + 1 << '\n';
                return 0;
            }
            stack.pop_back();
        }
    }
    cout << (stack.empty() ? 0 : stack.front() + 1) << '\n';
}
~~~
